WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.575
Hi, I'm Holly!

00:00:02.575 --> 00:00:05.460
SGLang can now keep its KV cache in four bits.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:06.037
Here's how.

00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:14.771
Store each cached value in four bits, and give every sixteen values

00:00:14.771 --> 00:00:17.969
their own scale, so an outlier only spoils its block.

00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:22.143
Count bits per value.

00:00:22.983 --> 00:00:24.234
Four bits for the value.

00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:28.725
Plus an eight-bit scale, shared by a block of B values.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.360
Set it equal to point five six two five, and B comes

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.593
out as exactly sixteen.

00:00:34.593 --> 00:00:36.135
The scale is already counted.

00:00:38.008 --> 00:00:42.408
Prefill computes in FP8, then packs keys and values into one pool:

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:44.975
four-bit values, and a scale per sixteen.

00:00:45.394 --> 00:00:47.522
Decode reads the four-bit cache directly.

00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:52.489
Fewer bytes per step: twenty-six to thirty percent more throughput at the same batch.

00:00:52.909 --> 00:00:58.044
Chunked prefill expands the cached prefix back into FP8, so prefill gets no speedup.

00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:03.425
Here is the post's own figure: one shared memory pool in the

00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:05.491
middle, and three attention paths around it.

00:01:05.911 --> 00:01:10.280
Inside the pool: packed four-bit values, one block scale per sixteen elements,

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.100
and a per-layer global scale.

00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:15.860
The benchmarked model has sixty-four layers.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.615
Forty-eight are gated DeltaNet, holding a fixed-size state the flag never touches.

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:25.784
Only the sixteen full-attention layers keep a per-token cache: thirty-two kibibytes a

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:28.158
token in FP8, eighteen in four-bit.

00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:34.309
At thirty-two K, FP8 fit forty-four requests, and four-bit fit seventy: every

00:01:34.309 --> 00:01:35.868
request the client offered.

00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:39.375
So the one point seven eight times went untested.

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:45.153
The 1.78x is a property of the cache pool, not the GPU:

00:01:45.153 --> 00:01:46.911
weights and workspace don't shrink.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:53.268
To recap: four-bit values with a scale per sixteen, a faster decode,

00:01:53.268 --> 00:01:55.695
and a cache only full attention keeps.

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:58.190
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:01:58.782
I'm Holly.

00:01:58.782 --> 00:01:59.079
Bye!
